# studies-archive-geometry-v2-2026-10

**The V2/CSG research record**, extracted from `geometry-v2` at `85e98bd1` (also tagged
`studies/geometry-v2-2026-10`). ~20 kLOC of study code that produced V2's numbers and that
`lucid/csg` cites 33 times. The branch still exists; this tarball is the readable copy, so that the
numbers quoted in csg's comments have a source you can open without checking out a 375-commit line.

**None of it runs against LUCiD v1.** There is no `lucid/csg` in the v1 tree, and the V2 re-entry is
deferred. Everything in here imports `lucid.csg` from the V2 branch, and a large fraction of it also
hard-codes a path into another checkout (see "What will not run", below).

## Layout

The tarball reproduces the V2 repository layout verbatim; nothing was renamed on the way out, so a
path quoted in a `lucid/csg` comment or in `RESULTS.md` resolves here unchanged.

| in this tarball | what it is | files |
|---|---|---|
| `toys_recon/` | the numbered gradient ladder t1–t117 + `harness.py` / `enginepmt.py` / `twopmt.py` | 125 |
| `toys/` | six closed-form reproductions of a claimed `sil_edge` reflection bias | 6 |
| `pool_bench/*.py`, `*.md`, `*.sh`, `*.json` | the benchmarks, boundary-estimator and closure studies, csg-vs-base parity, the waveform work, `RESULTS.md` (885 lines) | 212 |
| `pool_bench/twolevel/` | the rejected nested two-level grid, with its head-to-head harness | 10 |
| `pool_bench/out/diag/` | the 53 real diagnostic scripts that lived among the run outputs | 53 |
| `hessian_probe/` | the AD-vs-FD and NaN/Hessian audit of the **base** engine | 33 |
| `baseline_scripts/` | the April `tools.*` → `lucid.*` equivalence captures | 10 |
| `archive/` | `recon_drivers_snapshot.tar.gz` + `MD5SUMS.txt` + `PROVENANCE.md` | 3 |
| `s3df_jobs/` | the nray/energy/geometry scan submitters (cross-listed into A4) | 10 |
| `scripts/campaign_recon/` | the seeded Geant4 recon campaign | 33 |
| `lucid/csg/viz/` | the browser scene viewer, including 620 KB of vendored three.js | 6 |
| `lucid/csg/manifold.py`, `lucid/csg/occlusion_edges.py` | the two held-back estimators | 2 |
| `lucid/csg/SCATTERING_REFLECTION_DESIGN.md`, `docs/POOL_MARCHING.md`, 14 `docs/*.md` | the design record and the plan/migration notes | 16 |

**Not in here, deliberately:** the 198 run artefacts under `pool_bench/out/` (169 `.log`, 20 `.npz`,
10 `.json`, 7 under `out/slurm/`), 97 of which were swept in by a `git add -A`; and
`pool_bench/out/transport_old.py`, a superseded copy of `transport.py` that git still has.

## What was promoted out of here, and what was not

One thing was promoted into the LUCiD v1 tree:

* `hessian_probe/recon_nan_thorough.py` → **`tests/test_photon_step_nan.py`**, because
  `lucid/simulation/photon_step.py` rested its "verified NaN-free across ~3·10⁴ grad/Hessian evals"
  claim on scripts that all `chdir` to another checkout. The test is a real sweep over the same
  stress geometries, run against the v1 tree.

The rest of A12's promotions are **not done and are not in v1**: `harness.py` → `tests/csg/oracles.py`,
six numbered toys → `tests/csg/`, and `{csg_bench, full_gsweep, pool_tune, stress_K}.py` →
`benchmarks/csg/` all land inside `lucid/csg`'s own re-entry, which LUCiD v1 defers
(`phases/DEFERRED_V2.md`): v1 has no `lucid/csg`, no `tests/csg/` and no `benchmarks/`. **The four
benchmarks that would become `benchmarks/csg/` are here, at their V2 paths:**

| would-be `benchmarks/csg/` name | path in this tarball |
|---|---|
| `csg_bench.py` | `pool_bench/csg_bench.py` |
| `grid_sweep.py` | `pool_bench/twolevel/full_gsweep.py` |
| `pool_tune.py` | `pool_bench/pool_tune.py` |
| `stress_k.py` | `pool_bench/stress_K.py` |

They are benchmarks, not pytest: Slurm-only, GPU, and the `G=16` default that `lucid/csg/simulator.py`
quotes was measured by them on a 2080 Ti. Whoever re-enters them owes the docstring that GPU.

The two-level grid's two transferable rules are quoted below, so they survive even if nobody reads
`twolevel/README.md`:

> 1. **Pad candidate tables consumed inside a `while_loop` to a power-of-two row width.** XLA's
>    gather/tiling lowering swings identical arithmetic 2–8× at widths 12/13/14 (a "trim to realized
>    width" made the walk *slower*).
> 2. **`while_loop` for expensive bodies, fixed-length `scan` for trivial ones** — the fixed-length
>    always-worst-case tradeoff wins only when the per-iteration body is a few int ops (the cull
>    corridor), loses for interval tests.

## What is actually in each part, and what it is worth

`toys_recon/` is why csg's position gradient was ~280× noisier than FD, in 117 numbered steps across
ten arcs — **final verdicts only**: 72 of the scripts retract an earlier step, so no single docstring
in there is evidence. `harness.py` is the best-engineered thing in the sandbox: a numpy-only analytic
one-PMT truth, a "did the knob move the forward at all" guard, and a printer that refuses to quote a
ratio whose denominator is not resolved at 3σ. Its `subprocess git` provenance banner is the one part
that must not travel into a test.

`pool_bench/RESULTS.md` §1–29 is the measurement log behind every number quoted in `lucid/csg`'s
comments, including the `G=16` grid default; 164 of its 206 scripts appear in no document, their
result being the commit message. The nested two-level grid was measured **1.7–3.3× slower** than the
single-level candidate grid and rejected. All `twolevel/` scripts import from another checkout
(`LUCiD_pool`) and cannot run from a clone.

`out/diag/` holds the only NaN diagnostics (`t107_nan{,2,3}.py`), behind two default-path fixes.

`toys/` closes a **non**-bug: the estimator was never biased by reflection; the FD reference was too
imprecise. Its `README.md` still states the refuted explanation — read `FINDING.md`.

`hessian_probe/` is where "no `custom_vjp`, forward-mode Jacobians, AD Fisher" came from. **Caveat:
all 31 scripts `sys.path`-insert and `chdir` to `/sdf/group/neutrino/omara/LUCiD_hessian`, so they
measured another checkout, not the tree they sat in.** That is the whole reason
`tests/test_photon_step_nan.py` exists.

`baseline_scripts/` is dead on arrival (the `tools` package no longer exists); kept only so the
refactor's acceptance method is readable.

`scripts/campaign_recon/` is superseded by `analysis/paper/utils/pipeline.py`, which says so; 14 of
its 26 scripts hard-code ROOT files in other checkouts. Its `RESULTS.md` and `REVAL_RESULTS.md` are
cited by main's paper path, which does not track them — rescuing those two into
`analysis/paper/provenance/` is a separate, still-open item (`analysis/paper/` is held).

`archive/recon_drivers_snapshot.tar.gz` is a tarball of another worktree's dirty tree
(`LUCiD_recon` @ `19a282a4`, 349 modified/untracked entries). Its own `PROVENANCE.md` says the commit
hash does not reconstruct it: the tarball is the authoritative copy.

`lucid/csg/viz/` had no importer anywhere in the V2 snapshot (`rg` for `csg.viz` / `scene_to_viz` /
`build_html` outside `viz/` → 0). It drops every non-sensor sphere, ignores its documented `surface`
argument, and its assets are not package data, so a non-editable install breaks it. **Its 620 KB of
vendored three.js and `OrbitControls.js` carry no licence text** — if it ever returns it is
`tools/csg_viewer` outside `lucid/`, with a CDN import map and `integrity=` hashes.

`lucid/csg/manifold.py` and `occlusion_edges.py` are the chain-hold and occlusion-edge estimators that
V2.0 does not ship. `manifold.py` has **zero tests**, two estimators that coexist while `pinned_chain`
silently disables the hold, ~80 drifted duplicate lines, 27-argument functions, and a damped route
that is incomplete ("no rim flux yet"). `occlusion_edges.py`'s `edge_cn` has no caller anywhere and
cites a "t120" that never existed. Each re-enters as one PR with a measured verdict and a test.

The 16 design documents are session diaries that contradict the code in at least five places and
themselves in two. Read them as a record of what was being attempted, not as a specification.

## Uncommitted V2 work is NOT in this tarball

`git archive` captures refs, not working trees. At build time four worktrees sat on V2 commits with
dirty trees, and three of them touch material that is in here:

| worktree | HEAD | uncommitted work that bears on this asset |
|---|---|---|
| `geometry-v2` | `85e98bd1` | **722 untracked files, 146 MB.** 5 modified + 8 new `lucid/csg/*.py` and 4 `tests/csg/*.py` (a newer csg representation), and 710 files of a whole untracked design programme, `studies/geometry_v2/` — see below |
| `LUCiD_calib` | `85e98bd1` | `lucid/csg/manifold.py` +62/−10 and `occlusion_edges.py` +82 — **both held-back estimators are newer in that worktree than in here** |
| `LUCiD_pinfix` | `85e98bd1` | `lucid/csg/manifold.py` +7/−3 and `occlusion_edges.py` +82, differing from `LUCiD_calib`'s |
| `LUCiD_pool` | `cf5d9e9a` (`lucid-fast`, **not** `85e98bd1`) | 756 uncommitted entries, 516 under `toys_recon/` and 238 under `pool_bench/` — including two untracked toys, `e16_csg_tf32.py` and `e22_csg_factor.py` |

`geometry-v2:studies/geometry_v2/` is the largest of these and the one no inventory names, because
every inventory was taken from the ref and it is on no ref: **24 design documents** (`DESIGN_v0`…`v5`,
`CSG_REPRESENTATION.md`, `GDML_TREATMENT_v0..v2`, `CORE_MAPPING.md`, `FUNDAMENTALS{,_v2}.md`,
`PLAN{,_v2}.md`, …), 124 Python files across 21 topic directories (`bodies`, `cells`, `contracts`,
`edges`, `g4oracle`, `irproto`, `leaf_tests`, `mapping`, `materials_draft`, `position`, `repr`,
`scatter`, `structure_meas`, …), 24 GDML files and 138 MB of `corpus/` and `position/` fixtures. It is
a later and larger design effort than the 16 design documents this tarball ships, and it supersedes
several of them.

So: **before anyone reuses the two held-back estimators, diff them against `LUCiD_calib` and
`LUCiD_pinfix`**; before anyone treats the gradient ladder as complete, look at `LUCiD_pool`; and
before anyone reads the 16 design documents here as current, read `studies/geometry_v2/` first.

Nothing is lost — the worktrees are still on disk, and a read-only copy of all of it sits under
`/sdf/data/neutrino/omara/lucid_cleanup_exec/WU-10/d-v2-13/` — but it is not in this tarball, and
`git worktree remove` deletes untracked and ignored files.

## What will not run

* Everything needs `lucid.csg`, which exists only on `geometry-v2`.
* All 31 `hessian_probe/` scripts and all 9 `pool_bench/twolevel/` scripts `sys.path`-insert or
  `chdir` into another checkout (`LUCiD_hessian`, `LUCiD_pool`).
* 23 `pool_bench` scripts write their outputs **inside the repository**.
* 14 of 26 `scripts/campaign_recon/` scripts hard-code ROOT files in other checkouts.
* `baseline_scripts/` imports a `tools` package that no longer exists on any ref.
* The benchmarks are GPU-only and were measured on an RTX 2080 Ti.
